---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/abandoned-cart";
const title = "Shopify abandoned cart and checkout recovery";
const desc = "Build recovery around a known checkout, a useful message and a reliable purchase exit. Compare tools against the same recovery contract.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should the first recovery email send?",
    "answer": "Choose a delay that fits the buying process and event freshness. Document timer resets and purchase exits; no fixed delay works for every catalog."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every cart receive a discount?",
    "answer": "No. Clarify the product or checkout problem first. Test an approved offer against a comparable reminder-only policy and include its costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Shopify automate abandoned checkout email?",
    "answer": "Shopify documents abandoned-checkout automation in Shopify Messaging. Check the current settings, default subscriber audience and workflow requirements before adding another sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens when a variant sells out?",
    "answer": "Avoid promising unavailable inventory. Explain any changed cart, offer an appropriate current destination, and let the customer choose a separate restock signup."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">

<h2>Define what was abandoned</h2>
<p>An item added to a cart, a started checkout and an identifiable, eligible recipient are different states. Name the event your integration actually supplies; do not call every anonymous cart recoverable. Record the checkout or cart key, customer identity, event time, currency, variant lines and recovery destination. Keep missing identity and unavailable event data visible instead of counting them as failed email conversions.</p>
<p>Shopify documents <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">native abandoned-checkout automation in Shopify Messaging</a>. Its documented default audience is email-marketing subscribers. Review your store's settings and eligibility before adding an external flow; two active recovery senders can contact the same shopper.</p>
<h2>Write the recovery contract</h2>
<p>Choose an inactivity delay based on the buying process, then document what resets the timer. A custom configuration with compatibility questions may need more consideration than a familiar reorder. There is no universal first-send time. Start with a reminder that clarifies the product, delivery terms or next step rather than assuming a coupon fixes the reason for leaving.</p>
<p>Before every queued message, recheck purchase completion, current eligibility, suppression and whether the destination still works. Define whether a new checkout replaces the previous enrollment. A purchase in another session should exit the recovery journey when identity matching can establish it; unmatched purchases are an explicit integration limitation to investigate.</p>
<h2>Keep the cart truthful</h2>
<p>Use variant-level content, current price presentation and an actionable checkout link. If only part of a cart remains available, explain that clearly. Do not silently substitute a different size or component. If inventory cannot be checked reliably before sending, use less specific availability language and provide a current product destination rather than promising stock.</p>
<p>A sold-out line may justify offering a <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">restock signup</a>, but that signup is a separate customer choice. Neither a recovery email nor a stock alert reserves inventory. For subscription products, show cadence and recurring terms accurately instead of attaching a one-time offer that conflicts with the subscription.</p>
<h2>Resolve competing messages</h2>
<p>Write a precedence rule for checkout recovery, welcome offers and browse reminders. For example, a proposed policy could pause browse reminders while an eligible checkout journey is active, and resume only if the earlier interest is still relevant. Define expiry and release conditions so a stale checkout cannot suppress future campaigns forever.</p>
<p>Review support cases and unresolved payment failures separately. A payment error may need a helpful recovery route rather than repeated urgency. Keep order confirmations operational; adding promotional content does not solve an inaccurate recovery audience. If adding SMS, confirm channel eligibility and deduplicate offers across channels. An unopened email alone is not proof that a person should receive a text.</p>
<h2>Make the incentive decision explicit</h2>
<p>Begin with the product question or friction you are trying to solve. If testing an offer, finance should approve the margin floor, product exclusions, code stacking and expiry. Prior discount use is evidence of redemption, not proof a customer requires discounts. Do not train a rule on a prediction whose meaning the operator cannot explain.</p>
<p>Evaluate discounted recovery against a comparable reminder-only path where feasible. Count the offer cost and later refunds as well as the recovered order. A higher platform-attributed total cannot by itself show the offer caused extra purchases. Use the <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">attribution guide</a> for experiment design and the <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount workflow</a> for incentive ownership.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance scenarios</h2>
<p>These checks have not been executed here. Create controlled checkouts for a guest with a known email, a returning customer, a partially unavailable cart and a completed purchase before the scheduled send. Add duplicate events, changed permission and a competing welcome offer. Inspect the enrollment log, rendered lines, exit reason and destination for each case. A successful test message proves rendering, not event eligibility.</p>
<p>For a hypothetical keyboard configuration, ask whether a changed switch variant appears correctly and whether the recovery copy can link to compatibility help without asserting that the build is compatible. For a coffee subscription checkout, inspect recurring terms and prevent accidental stacking with an existing subscription benefit. These are different acceptance cases, not reported merchant outcomes.</p>
<h2>Compare a sender against the contract</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/apps">catalog</a> to find candidates, including Sequenzy and alternatives already under consideration. Request evidence for the exact checkout event, identity matching, delayed exits, variant rendering, inventory freshness, offer controls and logs in the intended plan. Record connector dependencies and custom work. No ranking here implies those capabilities have been tested.</p>
<p>Keep a recovery ledger: eligible checkouts, enrolled recipients, suppressed recipients and reasons, sends, linked orders, refunds and incentive cost. Compare results with equal observation windows and retain unmatched cases. Review complaints and support replies alongside recovery value; lowering friction is a different intervention from adding another touch.</p>

  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
